deep.reconstruction:
Setting. Λ = Z/4Z, M is a free Λ-module of rank 2, and M_0 = M/2M ≅ F_2^2. J = M_0^* is the 3-element set of nonzero vectors of M_0, so 𝔖_J ≅ Gl_{F_2}(M_0) ≅ S_3. I is a 4-element set, apparently the affine plane underlying M_0, so 𝔖_I ≅ AGL(M_0) = Gl(M_0)·M_0 ≅ S_4 and 𝔖_I^+ = A_4. 𝔷 = {±id_M} is the centre of Gl_Λ(M). Q is a 'square' structure, D_Q its dihedral automorphism group of order 8, and D_Q^+ ≅ C_4 its rotation subgroup.

Page content.

(1) Top NB: the anatomy of D_Q = Gl_Λ(M)/𝔖_I^+.
- D_Q has centre 𝔷_Q = {1, e_ω}, of order 2.
- It has exactly three subgroups of index 2: two Klein subgroups V_S = F_2^C and V_A = F_2^D, and the cyclic subgroup D_Q^+.
- Each of these minus the centre is a 2-element set, hence an F_2-torsor, which Grothendieck names intrinsically:
  - V_S∖𝔷_Q ≅ ω (≅ C_Q), matched to the character sg;
  - V_A∖𝔷_Q ≅ ω∧μ (the contracted product of the two torsors), matched to sg·det;
  - D_Q^+∖𝔷_Q ≅ μ = (Λ^2 M)^* = Or(M), the 2-element set of orientations, matched to det.
- This refines Corollaire 2 of the previous page: (Gl_Λ M)_ab ≅ (D_Q)_ab ≅ F_2 × F_2, with the three nontrivial characters det, sg, sg·det.
- He also notes Gl_Λ(M)' ≅ 𝔖_I × F_2 ≅ Aut(Δ), the full symmetry group (order 48) of the octahedron Δ whose vertex set is M^*/±1 = P^1(Z/4) (6 points). This is developed on the next page.
- The order count fits ' = Gl/𝔷 = PGL_2(Z/4), which acts faithfully on the 6 points of P^1(Z/4). The text below also identifies Ker(sg·det) ≅ 𝔖_I × F_2.

(2) Diagram (28): the lattice of distinguished subgroups of GL_2(Z/4) (order 96), with indices on the arrows. Orders:
- 1;
- 𝔷 (2);
- M_0 = Klein subgroup inside A_4 (4);
- F_2^ω (4);
- M_0×𝔷 ≅ Sl(M_0) (8);
- 𝔖_I^+ (12);
- M_0×F_2^ω ≅ Gl(M_0)-kernel = 1+2M_2 (16);
- 𝔖_I^+×𝔷 (24);
- 𝔖_I^+×F_2^ω, 𝔖_I×𝔷 (dotted) and Sl_Λ(M) (48);
- Gl_Λ(M) (96).
The brackets name quotients:
- Gl/(M_0×F_2^ω) ≅ 𝔖_J = Gl_{F_2}(M_0);
- Gl/𝔖_I^+ ≅ D_Q;
- Sl/𝔖_I^+ ≅ D_Q^+ ≅ (Sl_Λ M)_ab ≅ Λ^2 M ≅ Z/4;
- Sl/(M_0×𝔷) ≅ 𝔖_J^+ (sic).
The NB states that all arrows are injective and the five squares are cartesian.

(3) Splittings. After 'half-splitting' M, i.e. trivialising the torsor P_M:
- Gl_Λ(M) ≅ Gl_{F_2}(M_0)·(M_0×F_2^ω) ≅ (Gl_{F_2}(M_0)·M_0)·F_2^ω = 𝔖_I · F_2^ω;
- Ker(sg·det) ≅ 𝔖_I × F_2.
These isomorphisms depend on the chosen trivialisation.

Purpose. To give an intrinsic, choice-free description of the level-4 group GL_2(Z/4) acting on level-4 structures of elliptic curves (char ≠ 2), and of its octahedral geometry. This is the classical fact that X(4) ≅ P^1 has octahedral symmetry, with the 6 cusps as octahedron vertices.
